WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.480
JENNY BROWN: Welcome to Cloud
Security Basics, a series where

00:00:03.480 --> 00:00:07.020
we explain the ins and outs
of securing your application

00:00:07.020 --> 00:00:08.760
on Google Cloud.

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:09.850
Sound fun?

00:00:09.850 --> 00:00:12.130
Then stick around
because in this episode,

00:00:12.130 --> 00:00:14.100
you'll learn about
Cloud audit logging.

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:17.579
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:29.010 --> 00:00:33.010
[PHONE DIALING]

00:00:33.010 --> 00:00:36.490
BRAD ACTOR: Salutations,
Cloud detective.

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:38.740
Last episode, you
used service accounts

00:00:38.740 --> 00:00:41.410
to foil my grand attempt
to compromise your apps

00:00:41.410 --> 00:00:43.780
and scripts that allow
for automated access

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:45.730
into your system.

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:49.030
This time, I decided
to go old school.

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:52.480
One of your employees is logging
in in the middle of the night

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:55.600
and accessing things
they shouldn't be.

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.450
How do I know this?

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:01.630
Well, maybe it's because
you're not their only employer.

00:01:01.630 --> 00:01:05.170
The question is, in a
fully remote workplace,

00:01:05.170 --> 00:01:07.210
how are you ever
going to track down

00:01:07.210 --> 00:01:10.240
who the wayward employee is?

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:12.550
If you don't do
something soon, I just

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:15.370
might have to ask
our mutual employee

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:19.030
to make some unfortunate
changes to a slideshow.

00:01:19.030 --> 00:01:21.640
How embarrassing would it
be if all of your images

00:01:21.640 --> 00:01:25.930
were replaced with cats at the
next company-wide presentation?

00:01:25.930 --> 00:01:28.960
JENNY BROWN: All right,
so here's what we know.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:32.140
Admins are responsible for
coming up with solutions

00:01:32.140 --> 00:01:35.770
to the many ways users can
interact with their systems.

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:38.560
And in this case, we
need to track down

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:42.280
who's accessing files
when they shouldn't be.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:44.440
If this was at the
office, we could

00:01:44.440 --> 00:01:46.480
ask the security
guards to walk around

00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:49.340
and note if they see
anything suspicious.

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:52.210
And if we were at the
office, we would actually

00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:54.370
be able to look at
the security logs

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:57.460
to see whose keycard
was being used to access

00:01:57.460 --> 00:01:59.930
the building late at night.

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:01.670
Wait a minute.

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:04.070
That gives me an idea.

00:02:04.070 --> 00:02:07.640
See, Google Cloud provides
a lot of tools and services

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:11.000
to protect its users from
cases just like this.

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:13.220
And one of the most
important tools

00:02:13.220 --> 00:02:15.380
is called Google
Cloud Audit Logs,

00:02:15.380 --> 00:02:19.730
which lets teams easily
create and manage audit logs.

00:02:19.730 --> 00:02:22.790
Regardless of how carefully
we code or thoroughly

00:02:22.790 --> 00:02:26.190
debug a piece of software,
problems are going to come up.

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:29.060
And when they do,
it's almost impossible

00:02:29.060 --> 00:02:32.750
to determine why they happened
and figure out how to fix them

00:02:32.750 --> 00:02:34.670
unless there's a
trail of breadcrumbs

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:36.710
to follow along the way.

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:40.580
And security logging provides
that crucial trail of evidence.

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:43.700
So during an active
investigation or postmortem

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:46.670
analysis, having regular
logs to go through

00:02:46.670 --> 00:02:49.970
is critical to identify
the cause of the issue.

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:53.960
They help establish baselines
and find operational trends.

00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:55.910
Logging security
events that keep

00:02:55.910 --> 00:02:58.040
track of every
activity that happens

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:00.950
in the digital environment,
your organization

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:03.890
will monitor these logs
for any suspicious activity

00:03:03.890 --> 00:03:07.100
or unauthorized
access to the system.

00:03:07.100 --> 00:03:09.230
Whenever these
breaches are found,

00:03:09.230 --> 00:03:11.930
the data is moved to
a central database

00:03:11.930 --> 00:03:15.206
so that the security team
can then investigate them.

00:03:15.206 --> 00:03:19.670
Hmm, I need to
investigate this more.

00:03:19.670 --> 00:03:22.580
When it comes to Google's
Cloud Audit Logs,

00:03:22.580 --> 00:03:26.780
some types of logs, like
Admin Activity logs,

00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:30.140
are on by default and
can't be disabled.

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:34.040
Others, like Data Access
logs, are optional,

00:03:34.040 --> 00:03:37.340
off by default, and
cost money to use.

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:40.640
Logs that are not essential
are turned off by default

00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:43.370
to save users money because
the logs could take up

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:46.610
a large amount of data that
would apply against Google's

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:48.290
quotas and limits.

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:52.310
But depending on what you
need, Cloud Audit Logs

00:03:52.310 --> 00:03:55.910
provide many customization
options to suit an organization

00:03:55.910 --> 00:03:59.130
and fulfill all their
security logging needs.

00:03:59.130 --> 00:04:03.080
So these include the
aforementioned Admin Activity

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:06.200
audit logs, which
cover API calls

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:10.040
and admin actions that modify
how metadata for resources

00:04:10.040 --> 00:04:11.630
is configured.

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:13.880
Whenever VM
instances are created

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:17.660
or identities are changed,
a log record is kept.

00:04:17.660 --> 00:04:19.730
These logs are
automatically kept,

00:04:19.730 --> 00:04:23.570
don't cost users anything,
and can't be disabled.

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:28.220
Then there's Data Access audit
logs, which keep track of users

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:33.210
as they create, change, and read
data that's been user provided.

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:36.020
These do have costs
associated with them,

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:39.620
except for BigQuery's
Data Access audit logs.

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:42.500
And Data Access audit
logs aren't on by default

00:04:42.500 --> 00:04:45.320
because they can
store a lot of data.

00:04:45.320 --> 00:04:47.900
And then there's
System Event audit

00:04:47.900 --> 00:04:52.760
logs to track changes to how
resources are configured.

00:04:52.760 --> 00:04:55.730
These logs are triggered
by Google systems,

00:04:55.730 --> 00:04:59.150
don't cost anything,
and can't be disabled.

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:03.380
And finally, there's
Policy Denied audit logs.

00:05:03.380 --> 00:05:06.350
Those are created whenever a
Google Cloud service denies

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:10.070
access to a user or service
account because of a security

00:05:10.070 --> 00:05:12.020
policy violation.

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:14.120
These are on by
default but do have

00:05:14.120 --> 00:05:16.230
costs associated with them.

00:05:16.230 --> 00:05:19.920
So we've got a lot of log
information to sort through,

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:23.060
but it's important to
understand that audit logs have

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:29.090
no concept of suspicious events
and are often best-effort items

00:05:29.090 --> 00:05:32.300
that can be delayed
by up to five minutes.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:34.640
And still, once
your security team

00:05:34.640 --> 00:05:37.430
is aware of a suspicious
event, your team

00:05:37.430 --> 00:05:40.070
will need to have the
correct tools and data

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:42.590
to work with to
assess the situation

00:05:42.590 --> 00:05:45.170
and take action as appropriate.

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:49.550
And luckily in our case, we
turned on Data Access logs.

00:05:49.550 --> 00:05:52.700
So let's look at those and
see who's been calling what

00:05:52.700 --> 00:05:54.930
and when.

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:58.860
And by taking a look at who's
been accessing files they

00:05:58.860 --> 00:06:03.630
shouldn't have, eureka, I was
able to figure out who's been

00:06:03.630 --> 00:06:05.610
accessing files they shouldn't.

00:06:05.610 --> 00:06:08.700
BRAD ACTOR: Drats, you
figured that out so quickly,

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:12.060
although it does make me wonder
why a regular scan of activity

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:15.570
wasn't already part
of your process.

00:06:15.570 --> 00:06:17.490
Google Cloud has
tools that allow

00:06:17.490 --> 00:06:20.760
you to set up real-time
alerts and notifications.

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:23.370
And regardless of if
you use those or not,

00:06:23.370 --> 00:06:25.710
your company should
implement an audit process

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:27.960
that involves regularly
reviewing the logs

00:06:27.960 --> 00:06:30.460
for suspicious behavior.

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:32.610
JENNY BROWN: So there you
have it, another episode

00:06:32.610 --> 00:06:35.750
of Cloud Security Basics.

00:06:35.750 --> 00:06:39.410
Next episode, we'll be looking
into this so-called security

00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:40.590
breach.

00:06:40.590 --> 00:06:43.490
So stay tuned for the rest
of the Cloud Security Basics

00:06:43.490 --> 00:06:46.170
Series, because when
it comes to security,

00:06:46.170 --> 00:06:47.660
you can't let bad actors win.

00:06:47.660 --> 00:06:51.010
[MUSIC PLAYING]

